.TH ADECTEST 1
.SH NAME
adectest \- test audio(3)'s decode filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adectest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Adectest
decodes the audio track of
.B /lib/movies/av.mov
through
.BR /dev/audiodec :
.IR quicktime (2)
finds the coded samples in Limbo, each is written to the filter, and
the PCM is read back.
.PP
It then
.B measures the pitch
of that PCM and compares it against the tone the clip was built with.
That is the point of the test.
"Some bytes came back" would pass against a decoder producing noise,
silence, or the wrong sample rate; a square wave of a known frequency
will not.
Pitch is measured by counting sign changes, which for a square wave is
exactly two per period, and unlike a peak or an energy measure cannot
be fooled by silence or a DC offset.
.PP
The clip carries a 220Hz square wave; decoded and measured back it
comes out around 216Hz, the difference being what a lossy codec does to
a square wave's harmonics.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/adectest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR audio (3),
.IR quicktime (2),
.IR vstreamtest (1)
.SH BUGS
Requires a platform decoder, so it is meaningful only under
.BR emu-cocoa .
.B Emu-g
has no audio device at all.
.PP
One codec, one sample rate, one channel, one second of tone.
Nothing here plays the audio, so nothing here tests the device's output
path, and nothing tests synchronisation with video \(em for which the
durations
.IR quicktime (2)
reports would be the starting point.
.PP
The first packet a decoder emits is the encoder's priming and is
silence; the measurement skips the start of the stream rather than
accounting for the priming exactly.
